  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Daily showers and thunderstorms likely to \ncontinue into next week.\n\nThough repetitive, the pattern of afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms is likely to continue into next week. Southwesterly \nwinds from the Bermuda High will keep conditions warm and moist and \norographic based storms will return today. The flash flood risk will \nalso be extending its stay as PWAT values of 1.75-2.00\" will support \nhigh rainfall rates into today and possibly tomorrow (Sunday). The \nWeather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk of Excessive Rainfall \nalong and west of the Blue Ridge for today as well as a Marginal \nRisk for the North Carolina counties and Grayson Highland area. MRMS \nhas Soil Moisture at around 60-80% for locations that have had \nprevious flash flooding, particularly in Carroll, Patrick, Surry, \nand Western Greenbrier Counties. The threshold for flooding for \nthese areas is very low so these will be areas of concern for \ntoday's thunderstorm activity.\n\nA large cutoff low over Canada will have several troughs and waves \nmove around it, with some pieces intersecting our forecast area\nnext week. This weather pattern will also assist in \nguaranteeing diurnal thunderstorm activity. There are few \nfeatures that are worth of note. One is a signal of severe \nweather for the middle of next week. While model guidance \nstruggles on the timing, a decent shortwave and low level jet is\nprojected to move across the area as a low level surface high \npressure system over Bermuda is forecast to move eastward. \nWhile low and mid level winds will be more \nwesterly/northwesterly, wind gusts may reach up to 45 mph \nagainst the mountains and shear may be introduced to provide \nstorm organization. While CAPE will remain plentiful thanks to \nthe previously supplied warm and moist air from the south, the \nlapse rates will need to increase if there is to be any \nconfidence in severe weather development. This event is still \nseveral days away so this will be monitored in future forecasts.\nAnother system of note is a small but potent shortwave that \nforms over the Ozarks this afternoon. Despite the temporal \nproximity to this event, model guidance has specific differences\nin its evolution. This disturbance looks to stall and attempt \nto break off from the main flow before weakening and sending \nsome attenuated vorticity to the region by Sunday afternoon. The\nmost likely impact from this system is a source of lift for \nstorm development but it is worth noting due to the model \ndiscrepancies and the small risk of the wave completely \ndetaching itself and having downstream effects on next week's \nforecast. Finally, the large ridge over the southwestern CONUS \nwill weaken and move southeastward over Texas towards the end of\nnext week. Its position will transport shortwave ridge riders \nto our area and continue the daily precipitation chances going.",
